{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "JavaScript", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 9, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 3, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear when talking about JavaScript.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Explain briefly how different programming languages work.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.   Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob09re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "rusty", "type": "name"}, {"content": "when you are out of practice; impaired in skill or knowledge about something or a specific topic", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I have to prepare a presentation about that topic and I'm a little bit rusty on that. Can you tell me what JavaScript is?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob09aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "runtime", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the time a computer program takes to be executed", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It lets you change the structure of the web at runtime, and not during the compilation.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob09aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "core technology", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a technology that is essential for the functioning of the program", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  It is also one of the three core technologies on the World Wide Web, including HTML and CSS.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob09aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "open up ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to start something or to begin operation", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(phrasal verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: So, when you open up a web page, the browser has to fetch the embedded JavaScript.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob09aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "fetch ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to go after something and return with it", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: So, when you open up a web page, the browser has to fetch the embedded JavaScript.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob09aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "instantly ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "when something is done at once or very fast", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adverb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  Finally, it executes the code and all of the changes you make will instantly take effect. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob09aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Modal Verbs for Obligation and Advice", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<strong>Have to</strong> and <strong>must</strong> are modal verbs that express obligation. That means that it is mandatory to do something.</br></br>  <strong>Should</strong> is also a modal verb, but it is commonly used to express advice, something that is a good idea.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When you want to say that something is mandatory or that is necessary to do something, you can use <b>have to</b> or <b>must</b>.</br> </br> Follow this structure: </br> </br> <div class=\"center_text\">Subject + <b>have to/must</b> + <b>verb base form</b> + complement </div> </br> Example: </br> <i>I <b>have to</b> follow this guide to use JavaScript. </i></br> <i>She <b>must</b> use JavaScript on that page.</i></br> </br> <b>Note: </b></br> <i>When you use <b>have to</b> remember to change it to <b>has to</b> in the third person</i>. </br> </br> Example: </br> <i>She <b>has to tell</b> us how to use JavaScript. </i></br> </br> Remember that when you use modal verbs, you <i>don't</i> need to add \u201c-s\u201d in the third person. </br> </br> Example: </br> <i>Daniel <b>must</b> have the presentation ready for Monday. </i></br> </br> If you want to say that something is a good idea, or give some advice to somebody, you can use the modal <b>should</b>.</br> </br> Example: </br> <i>They <b>should</b> teach about programming languages. </i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You should tell me about the key features of JavaScript as well, don't you think so? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Well, yes. I have to tell you how it works. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "So when you open up a web page the browser has to fetch the embedded JavaScript.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Common Content Words and Expressions Used in JavaScript", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "When you use JavaScript, you may find some words that are common like: <strong>High-level, dynamic, standardize, specification, core technology, regular expressions</strong>.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "In case you get to find these words when using JavaScript or maybe reading about it, here you have the meaning of each one of them.</br></br>  <strong>High-level:</strong> When talking about computers, a high-level programming language is one that has strong abstraction from the details of the computer; it is easier to use and makes the process simpler.</br></br>  <strong>Dynamic:</strong> In computer science, a dynamic programming language is one that executes many programming behaviors at runtime, instead of doing it during compilation, as static programming languages do.</br></br>  <strong>Standardize:</strong> To make everything into conformity with a standard.</br></br>  <strong>Specification:</strong> Is a type of a\u00a0standard\u00a0which is often referenced by a\u00a0contract\u00a0or procurement document. It provides the necessary details about the specific requirements.</br></br>  <strong>Core technology:</strong> A technology that is an important part of something else; it is essential for the functioning of the program.</br></br>  <strong>Regular expression:</strong> It is a sequence of characters used to define a search pattern.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "List of Programming and Computer Science Terms", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "In computer science, we can find several terms that are important to know, such as <strong>Generic software, GML (Generalized Markup Language)</strong> and <strong>GPL (General Purpose Language).</strong>", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Here are the definitions for each one of them.</br></br>  Generic software: this is a kind of software that can be used for multiple purposes without having to modify it. </br></br>  GML: (Generalized Markup Language) It is a set of macros that adds tags to a text and defines what it is in terms of paragraphs, headers, lists, tables, etc.</br></br>  GPL: (General Purpose Language) This is when a programming language is designed for writing software in many application domains, not just one. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "content