ontent": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 3, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n4le03ob09re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<strong>Christian:</strong>  Hey, Danna! Is it true you know about JavaScript? Because I need help! I have to prepare a presentation about that topic and I'm a little bit rusty on that. Can you tell me what JavaScript is?</br>\n<strong>Danna:</strong> Sure, JavaScript is a type of general-purpose programming language standardized in the ECMAScript language specification. It is also one of the three core technologies on the World Wide Web, including HTML and CSS; we also consider it as generic software.", "image": "enpe7n4le03ob09re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "34000", "text": "<strong>Christian:</strong> You should tell me about the key features of JavaScript as well, don't you think so? </br>\n<strong>Danna:</strong> Well, some of the key features of JavaScript are that it uses a GML, which is Generalized Markup Language. It is high-level; it is also dynamic and can work with regular expressions. Most web browsers support JavaScript so you don't have to download any plug-ins. Besides that, when you work with JavaScript, it lets you change the structure of the web at runtime, and not during the compilation.", "image": "enpe7n4le03ob09re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "70500", "text": "<strong>Christian:</strong> Anything else I should know about JavaScript?</br>\n<strong>Danna:</strong> Well, yes. I have to tell you how it works.</br>\n<strong>Christian:</strong> Please do!</br>\n<strong>Danna:</strong> So, when you open up a web page, the browser has to fetch the embedded JavaScript; after that, and depending what you want the JavaScript to do, it will either execute as soon as the file loads or after a triggering event, like a click or certain amount of time. Finally, it executes the code and all of the changes you make will instantly take effect.  </br>\n<strong>Christian:</strong> So, if I go to a website and load it, the browser has a built-in interpreter that reads the JavaScript code found on the page and runs it, right?</br>\n<strong>Danna:</strong> Correct!", "image": "enpe7n4le03ob09re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le03ob09re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}